# Q1: Do matchup edges hold across eras?

**Question:** Do some teams consistently do better against specific opponents
than expected, and does that hold in every era, or does it depend on the players?

**Source:** FiveThirtyEight NBA Elo dataset (nbaallelo.csv)
https://github.com/fivethirtyeight/data/tree/master/nba-elo

**Files**
- q1_games_clean.csv: cleaned data, one row per game
- q1_matchup_cleaning.ipynb: cleaning through Google Colab

## Cleaning steps
- Kept NBA games only (dropped the ABA, a separate league, before it merged with NBA)
- Kept regular season only (playoff series repeat the same matchup up to 7 times)
- Kept 1979-80 season onward (3-point line added; start of the modern NBA)
- Dropped 19 neutral-site games (no home team)
- Removed duplicate rows (each game was listed twice, once from each team's side)
- Final result: 39,620 games

## Columns
game_id: unique ID for each game
year_id: season, labeled by the year it ended (2015 = 2014-15)
fran_id: team A's franchise ID
opp_fran: team B's franchise ID
game_location: H = home team, A = away team
pts: points scored by team A
opp_pts: points scored by team B
forecast: pre-game chance team A wins, based on Elo ratings (already relative to the league each season)
win: 1 if team A won, 0 if they lost
edge: win - forecast; positive = team A did better than expected
era: 1980-1994, 1995-2004, or 2005-2015
